---
// The flagship post. Direct answer first, real table, GEO-quotable blocks.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { client } from '../../data/client.js';

const tel = client.phone.replace(/\s/g, '');
const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How much do solar panels cost in Sheffield? 2026 prices',
  datePublished: '2026-07-28',
  dateModified: '2026-07-28',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: client.owner, jobTitle: 'Solar installer' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://loxleysolar.co.uk/#business' },
};
const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://loxleysolar.co.uk/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://loxleysolar.co.uk/blog/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Solar panel cost in Sheffield', item: 'https://loxleysolar.co.uk/blog/solar-panel-cost-sheffield/' },
  ],
};

const table = [
  ['Small terrace', '6', '£4,400 to £5,200', '£390 to £470'],
  ['Semi, the common case', '10', '£5,900 to £7,500', '£640 to £790'],
  ['Large semi or detached', '12', '£7,200 to £8,600', '£770 to £940'],
  ['Big detached, dual aspect', '14+', '£8,200 to £9,500+', '£900 to £1,100'],
];
---
<Base
  title="How Much Do Solar Panels Cost in Sheffield? 2026 Prices"
  description="Solar panels in Sheffield cost £4,400 to £9,500 installed in 2026 depending on system size. An MCS installer breaks down real prices and what moves them."
  current="/blog/"
  schema={[article, breadcrumbs]}
>
  <article>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <header class="article__head">
        <h1>How much do solar panels cost in Sheffield? 2026 prices</h1>
        <p class="article__meta">
          By {client.owner}, MCS installer, {client.name} · July 2026 <!-- TEMP author -->
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="article__body">
        <p>
          A solar installation in Sheffield costs £4,400 to £9,500 in 2026:
          that is 6 to 14 panels, installed, with scaffolding, MCS paperwork
          and export registration included. Add £2,800 to £4,500 for a
          battery. Those are installed prices from jobs we quoted this year,
          not a lead-generation site's teaser figures, and VAT on domestic
          solar is zero-rated until 2027. <!-- TEMP figures throughout -->
        </p>

        <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="costs">
          <caption>
            Installed, incl. scaffolding, MCS certificate, DNO and export
            registration. Your written quote is the real number; this table
            is for orientation.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">System</th>
              <th scope="col">Panels</th>
              <th scope="col">Installed price</th>
              <th scope="col">Year-one benefit</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {table.map(([house, panels, price, benefit]) => (
              <tr><td><strong>{house}</strong></td><td class="fig">{panels}</td><td>{price}</td><td>{benefit}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>

        <h2>What moves the price</h2>
        <p>
          Scaffolding first: a terraced street with no rear access costs more
          to wrap than a driveway semi. Then the roof itself: Sheffield's
          stone-built terraces often need longer anchors and more careful
          slating than a 1970s concrete-tile semi. Then the electrics: a
          tired fuse board adds a day and a few hundred pounds. Panel brand
          moves the price less than people expect; labour and access move it
          more.
        </p>

        <h2>Does a battery pay its way?</h2>
        <p>
          Sometimes. Without one, a working household uses under half of what
          the roof makes; the rest exports at around 15p while you buy
          evenings back at 27p. A battery closes that gap and pushes self-use
          towards 80 percent. On a 10-panel system that is roughly £250 to
          £350 a year of extra benefit against £2,800 to £4,500 of cost, so
          the battery case rests on how much you are home, your tariff, and
          whether you charge a car. We price it separately in every estimate
          so you can drop it without dropping the panels. <!-- TEMP figures -->
        </p>

        <h2>How to read a solar quote</h2>
        <p>
          A real quote names the panel count and layout drawn on your roof,
          the inverter model, the scaffolding, the MCS and DNO paperwork, and
          who registers your export payments. If any of those is missing, the
          price is not finished. Two red flags worth walking away from: a
          price that only holds "if you sign today", and a yield figure with
          no assumptions attached. Ask any installer, us included, for the
          MCS number and check it on the register before you pay a deposit.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens on the job</h2>
        <p>
          Scaffold goes up one day, panels and inverter the next, and the
          system is commissioned and generating before the scaffold comes
          down, usually inside the same week. The paperwork takes longer than
          the roof: allow three weeks for MCS, DNO and export registration to
          clear. You get the certificates, the layout drawing and the
          handover walkthrough, then the app takes over.
        </p>

        <div class="band">
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.35rem;">Want the number for your actual roof?</h2>
          <p style="color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--t-small);">
            Two bills and a postcode gets you a written estimate with the
            assumptions listed, inside two working days. <!-- TEMP -->
          </p>
          <div class="acts">
            <a class="btn btn--quote" href="/#quote">Get a quote</a>
            <a class="btn btn--call" href={`tel:${tel}`}>Call Dan</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
